From 7b542ec1fab0301f5acf62a3f7d4134391a0a026 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Codex <codex@openai.local>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 19:10:32 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] The judge dies on its environment, not its budget: torn
 frames and a commit ceiling

Measured over the whole 844MB of MT5-Gauntlet.log: 212 passes reached pre-warm, 163 reached
`Saved to`, 117 reached the survivor write. Of 168 tracebacks, 129 are ONE fault and 19 are a
second, and neither is inside the sealed judge.

THE COMMIT CEILING. GetPerformanceInfo reports CommitPeak == CommitLimit == 245.95GB exactly
(96GB RAM + 150GB page file). At the ceiling the allocation that fails first is a freshly
spawned pool worker importing numpy, which is why the log carries 77 BrokenProcessPool, 23
`OSError: handle is closed`, 17 MemoryError and 12 `ImportError: DLL load failed ... the paging
file is too small`. The judge picks 15 workers holding ~1.8GB of private bytes each -- ~30GB of
commit at spawn -- against pf_allocator/deepening_worker/descendants holding 43.5GB. C: has
911GB free, so the ceiling is raisable; that is a reboot of a live trading box and is REPORTED,
never taken unasked.

THE TORN FRAME, fixed here. `fetch_universe` and `expand_universe` wrote bar frames with
`to_parquet` straight onto the destination, which truncates it and refills it over seconds. The
sealed `_bars_for` reads those frames with no error handling, so a read landing in that window
raises `ArrowInvalid: Parquet magic bytes not found` out of `main()` and kills the whole sweep.
320 of 1,736 frames are rewritten in any five-minute window. `universe_registry.publish_frame`
writes beside the destination and renames, so a reader sees the old frame or the new one and
never half of one; a rename that loses to an open handle leaves the previous frame whole and
says so.

WHAT A STOPPED PASS COSTS, now published rather than assumed. The judge's per-cell BUILD is
already durable (content-addressed cache, temp + os.replace). Its VERDICTS are not: the build
cursor, seen-cells, the gate ledger, universal_gates_external.json and UNIVERSAL_SURVIVORS.json
are one tail after `run_gauntlet` returns -- the same shape that discarded 65 passing cells.
Making a verdict durable per cell means writing inside `run_gauntlet`, which is in the SEALED
file. NOT ATTEMPTED. `scripts/gauntlet_guard.py` instead measures the tail's width, the commit
headroom, the integrity of every frame the judge will read, and the live pass's margin to its
window, into reports/GAUNTLET_PASSES.json -- so "the judge finishes now" is a reading.

Also: the two stale 1-arg `_auto_leg` stubs against HEAD's 2-arg signature, and the SIM105 at
hourly_cycle.py:1784. The sealed judge is untouched.

